	"capturedFrom": "CONSTRUCTED, from the wire contract settled for the interrupted-vs-failed workstream (verified 2026-09-28 against local-operator origin/main 11fc505e4 and this repository's origin/main 3a46e6c987). No daemon session was read: every frame here is built from the shapes the published contract names, so the fixture is stable across captures of both trees.",
	"note": "The two evidence states the operator reported: a steering skip (the batch tail the harness drops when steering redirects, `Tool call skipped: interrupted by steering.`) and a call the user stopped mid-flight. `skip.live` is the live path (an announcement the viewer watched, then the terminal frame); `skip.durable` is what a reload paints for the same call (the synthetic result the loop stores, `details.__fault: \"skipped\"`); `stop` is the end-event shape of an abort (`details.__fault: \"aborted\"`, the marker `loop.py:3979`'s backfill carries once the core half lands); `failure` is the control - a call that really ran and failed (`details.__fault: \"execution\"`), unchanged by this change in both trees. `turnCounts` reuses the skipped call's durable row inside a three-call turn, so the fold header and the turn foot can be read on one frame. The `not_run_kind: \"skipped\"` field on `skip.live.verdict` is the new wire field (core PR); every other frame uses only fields that ship today, and `skip.live.announcement` deliberately carries no kind and no reason - dictation frames never do.",
	"epoch": "Every `ts` here is epoch SECONDS, the unit the desktop history contract states; the live frames carry `started_at_epoch` in seconds as the wire does.",
